/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/pre-testing.Rmd:2:title: "Pre-Testing in a DiD Setup using the did Package"
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/pre-testing.Rmd:7:  %\VignetteIndexEntry{Pre-Testing in a DiD Setup using the did Package}
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/pre-testing.Rmd:31:This vignette provides a discussion of how to conduct pre-tests in DiD setups using the **did** package.
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/pre-testing.Rmd:33:* One appealing feature of many DiD applications with multiple periods is that the researcher can pre-test the parallel trends assumptions.
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/pre-testing.Rmd:37:    * Importantly, this is just a pre-test; it is different from an actual test.  Whether or not the parallel trends assumption holds in pre-treatment periods does not actually tell you if it holds in the current period (and this is when you need it to hold!).  It is certainly possible for the identifying assumptions to hold in previous periods but not hold in current periods; it is also possible for identifying assumptions to be violated in previous periods but for them to hold in current periods.  That being said, we view the pre-test as a piece of evidence on the credibility of the DiD design in a particular application.
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/pre-testing.Rmd:39:* In this vignette, we demonstrate that the approach used in the **did** package for pre-testing may work substantially better than the more common "event study regression".
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/pre-testing.Rmd:41:## Common Approaches to Pre-Testing in Applications
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/pre-testing.Rmd:43:By far the most common approach to pre-testing in applications is to run an **event-study regression**.  Here, the idea is to run a regression that includes leads and lags of the treatment dummy variable such as
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/pre-testing.Rmd:56:And $\mu_l$ is interpreted as the effect of treatment for different lengths of exposure to the treatment.  Typically, $\mu_{-1}$ is normalized to be equal to 0, and we follow that convention here.  It is common to interpret estimated $\mu_l$'s with $l < 0$ as a way to pre-test the parallel trends assumption.
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/pre-testing.Rmd:68:#### Best Case Scenario for Pre-Testing
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/pre-testing.Rmd:70:First, let's start with a case where an event study regression is going to work well for pre-testing the parallel trends assumption
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/pre-testing.Rmd:152:You will notice that everything looks good here.  The pre-test performs well (the caveat to this is that the standard errors are "pointwise" and would be better to have uniform confidence bands though this does not seem to be standard practice in applications).
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/pre-testing.Rmd:188:Contrary to event study regressions, pre-tests based on group-time average treatment effects (or based on group-time average treatment effects that are aggregated into an event study plot) **are still valid even in the presence of selective treatment timing**.
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/pre-testing.Rmd:318:Even when one includes covariates to estimate group-time average treatment effects, pre-tests based only on group-time average treatment effects can fail to detect some violations of the parallel trends assumption.  To give an example, suppose that the only covariate is binary variable for an individual's sex.  Pre-tests based on group-time average treatment effects could fail to detect violations of the conditional parallel trends assumption in cases where it is violated in one direction for men and in the other direction for women.
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/pre-testing.Rmd:320:The `did` package contains an additional pre-test for the conditional parallel trends assumption in the `conditional_did_pretest` function.
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/extensions.Rmd:175:      # pre-test, we need to adjust the base period)
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/multi-period-did.Rmd:91:2. There are only two time periods.  This is the canonical case (2 periods, one group becomes treated in the second period, the other is never treated).  In this case, under parallel trends an no-anticipation, $\alpha$ is going to be numerically equal to the $ATT$.  In other words, in this case, even though it looks like you have restricted the effect of participating in the treatment to be the same across all units, TWFE exhibits *robustness* to treatment effect heterogeneity.  Unfortunately, this robustness to treatment effect heterogeneity does not continue to hold when there are more periods and groups become treated at different points in time.
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/multi-period-did.Rmd:97:* newly treated units relative to ``never treated'' units (good!)
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/multi-period-did.Rmd:146:This is a natural extension of the parallel trends assumption in the two periods and two groups case.  It says that, in the absence of treatment, average untreated potential outcomes for the group first treated in time $g$ and for the "never treated" group would have followed parallel paths in all post-treatment periods $t \ge g$.
/opt/homebrew/lib/R/4.5/site-library/did/doc/multi-period-did.Rmd:148:Note that the aforementioned parallel trend assumption rely on using the ``never treated'' units as comparison group for all "eventually treated" groups. This presumes that (i) a (large enough) "never-treated" group is available in the data, and (ii) these units are "similar enough" to the eventually treated units such that they can indeed be used as a valid comparison group. In situations where these conditions are not satisfied, one can use an alternative parallel trends assumption that uses the **not-yet treated** units as valid comparison groups. 
