1010-<p><img src="data:image/png;base64," alt width="90%" style="display: block; margin: auto;" /></p>
1011:<p>These continue to be simultaneous confidence bands for dynamic
1012-effects. The results are broadly similar to the ones from the group-time
1013-average treatment effects: one fails to reject parallel trends in
1014-pre-treatment periods and it looks like somewhat negative effects of the
1015-minimum wage on youth employment.</p>
1016:<p>One potential issue with these dynamic effect estimators is that the
1017-composition of the groups changes with different lengths of exposure in
1018-the event study plots. For example, for the group of states who
1019-increased their minimum wage in 2007, we can only identify the
1020-instantaneous average effect of the minimum wage (<span class="math inline">\(e=0\)</span>), whereas for states that raised
--
1024-the effects for all three groups, but this is not the case when <span class="math inline">\(e=1,2,3\)</span>. If the effects of the minimum
1025-wage are systematically different across groups (here, there is not much
1026-evidence of this as the effect for all groups seems to be close to 0 on
1027-impact and perhaps becoming more negative over time), then this can lead
1028:to confounding dynamics and selective treatment timing among different
1029-groups.</p>
1030-<p>One way to combat this is to balance the sample by (i) only including
1031-groups that are exposed to the treatment for at least a certain number
1032:of time periods and (ii) only look at dynamic effects in those time
1033-periods. In the <code>did</code> package, one can do this by specifying
1034-the <code>balance_e</code> option. Here, we set
1035-<code>balance_e = 1</code> – what this does is to only consider groups
1036-of states that are treated in 2004 and 2006 (so we can compute
1037-event-study-type parameters for them for <span class="math inline">\(e=0,1\)</span>), drops the group treated in 2007
1038-(as we can not compute the event-study-type parameter with <span class="math inline">\(e=1\)</span> for this group)), and then only looks
1039-at instantaneous average effects and the average effect one period after
1040-states raised the minimum wage.</p>
